      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2304776279,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "So why does `nstime_copy()` exist as a function that copies individual elements? I *suppose* on a 64-bit platform it might do one fewer copy.  Gven that various versions of the C standard say in 6.2.6.1 \"General\" that \"When a value is stored in an object of structure or union type, including in a member object, the bytes of the object representation that correspond to any padding bytes take unspecified values.\", with a footnote explaining that this means you are not required to copy those padding bytes, a structure assignment *could* be implemented as a member-by-member assignment, so there's no *guarantee* that it would take one fewer copy.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-01-19T05:30:48.038Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-01-19T12:48:00.393Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 356356669,
