        {
          "id": 2304879528,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "I'm not sure why it was introduced. The only theoretical case that comes to mind with the C standard is if for some reason the `nstime_t` struct were given a const member that could only be set at initialization time, in which case structure assignment (which works as member-by-member assignment) would fail, and `nstime_copy()` would have to know to copy all the members except for whichever one was set at struct creation time.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
